+        'A near miss is an event that could have caused harm but didn’t: a pallet falling where, by luck, nobody stood. Reporting near misses gives you lessons for free.\n\nThe Heinrich pyramid (1931): for every serious injury, 29 minor ones and 300 events without injury. Bird later published 1 : 10 : 30 : 600: one serious injury, 10 minor ones, 30 with only property damage, 600 near misses.',
+        'The limits: the ratios vary widely, and serious and fatal events often have different causes than cuts and sprains. Safety people call them SIF: serious injuries and fatalities.\n\nBP’s Texas City refinery, where 15 people died in an explosion in 2005, had good figures for personal injuries. So look specifically at what can cause serious harm: released energy, work at height, vehicles, failed isolations.',
         'Learning without blame. Most incidents involve normal people in a system that made the mistake easy: poor design, time pressure, unclear procedures.\n\nA just culture (James Reason, Sidney Dekker) separates honest mistakes, which you learn from, from reckless behaviour, which has consequences. Punish people for reporting and the reports stop, while the risks stay.',
